-- abrego-garcia-leaves-us-spain-20261127 (news; USA; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'abrego-garcia-leaves-us-spain-20261127', 'news', 'USA',
'Abrego Garcia leaves US for Spain after legal fight',
'Abrego Garcia leaves US for Spain after legal fight',
'Kilmar Abrego Garcia left the US voluntarily for Spain on 24 November, landing in Madrid the next morning after a 20-month immigration legal battle.',
'Kilmar Abrego Garcia left the US voluntarily for Spain on 24 November, landing in Madrid the next morning after a 20-month immigration legal battle.',
$$<p>Kilmar Abrego Garcia, the Salvadoran man whose erroneous deportation became the seminal case in President Donald Trump's immigration crackdown, left the US voluntarily for Spain on Tuesday 24th November and arrived in Madrid the following morning.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Person</td><td>Kilmar Abrego Garcia, 31, Salvadoran national</td></tr>
<tr><td>Left the US</td><td>Tuesday 24 November 2026, Washington Dulles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arrived</td><td>Wednesday 25 November, Madrid-Barajas Airport</td></tr>
<tr><td>Visa</td><td>Tourist visa, up to 90 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Court order</td><td>Judge Paula Xinis, 20 November, unsealed 25 November</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Abrego Garcia left on a commercial United Airlines flight from Washington Dulles International Airport on 24 November, after agents removed the GPS ankle monitor he had worn for months, according to <a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2026/11/25/kilmar-abrego-garcia-speaks-out/92958861007/">USA Today</a>. He landed in Madrid early on 25 November with his wife, Jennifer Vasquez Sura, who is an American citizen, and his brother. He entered Spain on a tourist visa, allowing a visit of only up to 90 days, <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/politics/kilmar-abrego-garcia-self-deporting-to-spain?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> reported. There was no indication he entered under any formal agreement with the Spanish authorities.</p>

<p>The departure was arranged under an order by US District Judge Paula Xinis, signed on 20 November and unsealed five days later, requiring the government to remove his ankle monitor and allow his unimpeded departure to the country of his choice, and barring officials from diverting him anywhere else. The Department of Homeland Security announced his departure on Tuesday night, with Secretary Markwayne Mullin writing that he was delighted by the move to self-deport, according to <a href="https://www.wncw.org/2026-11-25/kilmar-abrego-garcia-leaves-u-s-voluntarily-for-spain">NPR</a>. His lawyer, Simon Sandoval-Moshenberg, said he left the United States a free man on a commercial aircraft, to a country he chose, and could move freely around the world.</p>

<p>Abrego Garcia was arrested in 2025, mistakenly deported to El Salvador despite a judge's order, and held in the CECOT mega-prison before being brought back to the United States, where he faced criminal charges in Tennessee. The government had threatened to deport him to Uganda and later to Liberia, on an ICE Air flight in shackles, if he did not accept a guilty plea. Speaking at a virtual news conference on 25 November, he said he was happy because this time he was leaving with his freedom, and not in the inhumane manner of his first removal.</p>

<h2>What the Abrego Garcia Spain move means</h2>

<p>The departure closes, for now, a case that became the most recognisable symbol of the administration's mass deportation campaign, but on Abrego Garcia's terms rather than the government's. He obtained the result the government had offered him in exchange for a coerced guilty plea, his lawyer said, without pleading guilty. The government, for its part, is presenting the exit as a self-deportation under a removal order, and says he will not be allowed back into the United States.</p>

<p>His lawyer called the absence temporary, saying Abrego Garcia is waiting out the end of this administration. As the husband of a US citizen and the father of a young, severely disabled US citizen child, he is eligible to return as a lawful permanent resident. However, he would first need waivers from the State Department that the family does not expect the Trump administration to grant. He is scheduled to speak at several events in Spain and other countries in the days ahead. More on US immigration is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/h-1b-100000-fee-blocked-november-2026-20261121">H-1B $100,000 fee blocked</a> report, and the latest from the wider world is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say where Abrego Garcia will be living when his 90 day tourist visa expires (or indeed whether Spain will grant him any longer term status). They don't say anything about the criminal charges he faces in Tennessee and whether those will be pursued by the US government while he is overseas. There is no information as to whether his wife and child will stay in Spain with him or whether they will return to the US.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.wncw.org/2026-11-25/kilmar-abrego-garcia-leaves-u-s-voluntarily-for-spain">NPR</a> for the departure, the arrival and the DHS statement; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/25/kilmar-abrego-garcia-self-deports-to-spain-after-lengthy-us-legal-battle">Al Jazeera</a> for the background of the mistaken deportation; <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/politics/kilmar-abrego-garcia-self-deporting-to-spain?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the tourist visa and the press conference; <a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2026/11/25/kilmar-abrego-garcia-speaks-out/92958861007/">USA Today</a> for the court order and the lawyer's account of the exit terms.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Where did Abrego Garcia go?</h3>
<p>Spain. He left the US on Tuesday 24th November 2026 and arrived at Madrid-Barajas airport on Wednesday morning, entering on a tourist visa valid for 90 days.</p>

<h3>Was he deported to Spain?</h3>
<p>No, he voluntarily left on a commercial flight to a country of his own choosing under a court order which precluded the Government from diverting him. The Government refers to it as a self-deportation under a removal order.</p>

<h3>Can he go back to the US?</h3>
<p>His lawyer says he is eligible to return as a lawful permanent resident, but would need State Department waivers that the family does not expect the current administration to grant.</p>
