-- cairo-tanker-fire-31-killed-ring-road-20261127 (news; Egypt; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'cairo-tanker-fire-31-killed-ring-road-20261127', 'news', 'Egypt',
'Cairo tanker fire kills 31 on Ring Road, driver held',
'Cairo tanker fire kills 31 on Ring Road, driver held',
'Cairo tanker fire on the Ring Road killed at least 31 people and injured 21, after a car struck the tanker and it overturned in rush hour.',
'Cairo tanker fire on the Ring Road killed at least 31 people and injured 21, after a car struck the tanker and it overturned in rush hour.',
$$<p>Cairo tanker fire on the Ring Road killed at least 31 and injured 21 others on Wednesday 25th November. The tanker overturned after being struck by a car during the evening rush hour.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Ring Road, Al-Warraq, Giza, near Cairo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>Wednesday 25 November 2026, about 6.30pm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Killed</td><td>At least 31</td></tr>
<tr><td>Injured</td><td>21, later updated to 26 by the Health Ministry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vehicles destroyed</td><td>27, including cars and motorcycles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Car struck the tanker, which overturned and caught fire</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The tanker (carrying petroleum products) went up in flames around 6.30pm on an elevated section of the Ring Road above Tunnel 10 in Al-Warraq (Giza), on the western outskirts of Cairo, according to <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/25/middleeast/cairo-egypt-tanker-fire-latam-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a>. The fire spread to the traffic passing the tanker and 27 vehicles were completely destroyed. The Health Ministry has put the death toll at 31 and 21 injured (preliminary figures), <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/world/middle-east/article/egypt-fire-cairo-fuel-tanker-q029bnbfr">The Times</a> reports. Some of the injured are in critical condition with burns covering 70-80% of their bodies.</p>

<p>Prosecutors said a car hit the back of the tanker, causing it to overturn and catch fire, and that a suspect had been arrested, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/egyptians-mourn-demand-accountability-after-deadly-tanker-explosion-kills-31-2026-11-26/">Reuters</a> reported. On Thursday, prosecutors ordered the detention of a 21-year-old driver suspected of causing the collision. They sent him for forensic testing to determine whether he had taken drugs, according to <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/26/egypt-detains-driver-over-cairo-fuel-tanker-fire-that-killed-31">Al Jazeera</a>. The driver told police he was speeding between lanes when he hit the tanker on its right rear, lost control and went right as one of the tanker's tyres blew, which caused it to go into the concrete wall of the highway.</p>

<p>By Thursday morning traffic was again moving slowly past the ash and charred tyres on the stretch of road where the blast occurred. Egyptian lawmakers were calling for accountability and Cairo residents were mourning the dead as another one of the string of transport disasters which has led to a surge in public anger. Egypt recorded 5,829 road deaths in 2025 (up 10.8% on the previous year) according to official statistics agency CAPMAS, as reported by Reuters.</p>

<h2>What the Cairo tanker fire means</h2>

<p>The fire was the deadliest of a series of recent accidents on Egypt's roads, coming less than three weeks after a train collision, and it has renewed questions about how fuel tankers and other heavy vehicles are regulated on highways that are often poorly policed. The Ring Road is a road that effectively encircles a metropolitan area of between 20 and 30 million people. A crash at rush hour on a raised section of road meant there was no place for the drivers to go once the fire started. The Health Minister visited the victims in hospital and DNA work was ongoing to identify the deceased.</p>

<p>The detention of the driver provides prosecutors with a clear avenue of investigation. It doesn't however resolve the broader questions which are being asked by the legislature about road safety enforcement and the state of the vehicles in question. The tanker in question was owned by a private contractor and was en route from El-Marg in Cairo when it was struck. More on the fires and the transport disasters in the region can be found in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/strait-of-hormuz-oil-tankers-fire-20261124">Strait of Hormuz oil tankers fire</a> report and the latest from the wider world is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't agree on the final figure for the injured. CNN and the Health Ministry on Wednesday had 21 injured. Al Jazeera on Thursday had the Health Ministry's figure at 31 dead and 26 injured so the injured number could well be different. No one knows when the driver will be charged or what the drug tests revealed. No one knows whether the tanker was roadworthy or whether any company is going to be held to account. The only official cause given is that of the prosecutors which is the collision.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/25/middleeast/cairo-egypt-tanker-fire-latam-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the death toll, the location and the 27 vehicles; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/26/egypt-detains-driver-over-cairo-fuel-tanker-fire-that-killed-31">Al Jazeera</a> for the driver's detention and the updated injured figure; <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/egyptians-mourn-demand-accountability-after-deadly-tanker-explosion-kills-31-2026-11-26/">Reuters</a> for the prosecutors' account and the road death statistics; <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/world/middle-east/article/egypt-fire-cairo-fuel-tanker-q029bnbfr">The Times</a> for the preliminary toll and the rush hour timing.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How many people died in the Cairo tanker fire?</h3>
<p>At least 31 people were killed. The Health Ministry described the toll as preliminary, so it could still rise as victims are identified.</p>

<h3>What caused the fire?</h3>
<p>Prosecutors say a car struck the rear of a fuel tanker on the Ring Road, a tyre burst, and the tanker hit the concrete wall, overturned and caught fire. A 21-year-old driver has been detained pending investigation.</p>

<h3>Where did the crash actually occur?</h3>
<p>On an elevated section of the Cairo Ring Road above Tunnel 10 in the Al Warraq area of Giza (on the western edge of the capital), approximately 6.30pm on Wednesday 25th November.</p>
