<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/25/middleeast/cairo-egypt-tanker-fire-latam-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the death toll, the location and the 27 vehicles; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/26/egypt-detains-driver-over-cairo-fuel-tanker-fire-that-killed-31">Al Jazeera</a> for the driver's detention and the updated injured figure; <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/egyptians-mourn-demand-accountability-after-deadly-tanker-explosion-kills-31-2026-11-26/">Reuters</a> for the prosecutors' account and the road death statistics; <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/world/middle-east/article/egypt-fire-cairo-fuel-tanker-q029bnbfr">The Times</a> for the preliminary toll and the rush hour timing.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How many people died in the Cairo tanker fire?</h3>
<p>At least 31 people were killed. The Health Ministry described the toll as preliminary, so it could still rise as victims are identified.</p>

<h3>What caused the fire?</h3>
<p>Prosecutors say a car struck the rear of a fuel tanker on the Ring Road, a tyre burst, and the tanker hit the concrete wall, overturned and caught fire. A 21-year-old driver has been detained pending investigation.</p>

<h3>Where did the crash actually occur?</h3>
<p>On an elevated section of the Cairo Ring Road above Tunnel 10 in the Al Warraq area of Giza (on the western edge of the capital), approximately 6.30pm on Wednesday 25th November.</p>
