(gen_random_uuid(), 'cartagena-hostages-rescued-supermarket-20261127', 'news', 'Colombia',
'Cartagena hostages rescued after supermarket siege',
'Cartagena hostages rescued after supermarket siege',
'Cartagena hostages rescued as Colombian forces freed all hostages from a supermarket on 25 November, ending a 17-hour siege.',
'Cartagena hostages rescued as Colombian forces freed all hostages from a supermarket on 25 November, ending a 17-hour siege.',
$$<p>Cartagena hostages rescued after Colombian security forces freed all the hostages from a supermarket on Wednesday 25th November, the result of a 17 hour siege which had started following a failed attempt to rob an armoured vehicle.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Exito supermarket, La Castellana, Cartagena</td></tr>
<tr><td>Siege began</td><td>Tuesday 24 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Siege ended</td><td>Early Wednesday 25 November, forces stormed the store</td></tr>
<tr><td>Duration</td><td>About 17 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hostages</td><td>About 30 initially reported; police later said 76 in total</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outcome</td><td>All hostages rescued; one captor killed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Armed men with their faces covered seized the Exito supermarket in Cartagena on Tuesday, after a failed attempt to hold up an armoured truck at the shopping centre, according to <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/24/hostage-situation-reported-in-colombia-as-armed-men-flee-into-supermarket">Al Jazeera</a>. Security camera operators alerted police to the robbery attempt, and the would-be robbers took refuge in the nearby store, taking staff and customers hostage. Images shared by local media showed people kneeling inside the supermarket with their hands bound, while police snipers took up positions on surrounding roofs.</p>

<p>President Abelardo de la Espriella said the attackers initially held about 30 people and had released at least five older women during the standoff. Several explosions were heard before security forces entered the building early on Wednesday, where about 20 people were believed to be held, according to <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/world/colombia-hostages-rescued-after-forces-storm-cartagena-supermarket-3013959">Arab News</a>. De la Espriella, who arrived in the city wearing fatigues and a sidearm, said in a video posted on social media that in an extraordinary joint operation between the National Police and the Armed Forces, all the hostages were rescued, and one captor was killed. Cartagena's mayor said all the hostages were safe and sound and would reunite with their families before undergoing medical checks.</p>

<p>Colombian police later said 76 people were held hostage during the siege, more than double the number initially reported, according to <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/world/colombia-police-reveal-76-hostages-were-caught-in-supermarket-siege-3014058">Arab News</a>. Local media reported that the gunmen had demanded nearly $2 million, two armoured vehicles and safe passage to Barranquilla, about 130 kilometres to the north-east. However, those reports could not be independently verified. The siege unfolded on the tenth anniversary of Colombia's peace accord with FARC guerrillas, and amid a wave of attacks by rebel groups on government targets.</p>

<h2>What the Cartagena hostages rescued operation means</h2>

<p>The rescue is a triumph for the security forces and for De la Espriella who has just been elected president and was at the scene to take charge of the operation. It also underlines how fast a street robbery can escalate into a full blown hostage situation in a tourist city. Cartagena is a Caribbean port city famous for its walled colonial old town, its beach resorts and its cruise ship tourism. The siege held the city for the whole of one day with police and military on the outside of the supermarket and the streets effectively sealed off.</p>

<p>De la Espriella was very quick to rule out any involvement of guerrillas or paramilitaries. The attackers were just common criminals, caught off guard, with no way out and so they ended up kidnapping a group of people. That's an important distinction to make given the hostage crisis came as Colombia faces a worsening security situation, and the fact that this incident from the rebel attacks that are occurring elsewhere in the country. More on the Americas in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/us-returns-12-billion-venezuela-oil-proceeds-20261117">US returns $12bn in Venezuela oil proceeds</a> report and the wider world in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources are at odds as to how many people were taken. One report was of about 30, another of about 20 (the number inside when the forces went in), and police later said 76 in total, so the full picture is no confirmation of any ransom demand from the sources and they give no details as to the gunmen other than that one was killed. There is no information as to whether any of the hostages were injured and we don't know what charges the remaining captors will face.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/11/24/hostage-situation-reported-in-colombia-as-armed-men-flee-into-supermarket">Al Jazeera</a> for the start of the siege and the president's statement; <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/world/colombia-hostages-rescued-after-forces-storm-cartagena-supermarket-3013959">Arab News</a> for the storming, the rescue and the mayor's comments; <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/world/colombia-police-reveal-76-hostages-were-caught-in-supermarket-siege-3014058">Arab News</a> for the revised figure of 76 hostages.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How many hostages were in Cartagena?</h3>
<p>Approx 30 people were initially reported to have been held and it was thought that about 20 were inside the store when the forces stormed it. Police later confirmed that a total of 76 people had been taken during the 17 hour siege.</p>

<h3>How did the Cartagena siege end?</h3>
<p>Security forces stormed the Exito supermarket early on Wednesday 25th November following a number of explosions being heard. All the hostages were successfully rescued and one of the captors was killed.</p>

<h3>Why did the gunmen take the hostages?</h3>
<p>Police say they were common criminals who had gone in to rob an armoured vehicle and once the police were called they'd fled into the supermarket. The President has ruled out any involvement by guerrillas or a paramilitary group.</p>
