-- israel-gaza-hamas-officials-killed-20261127 (news; Global; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'israel-gaza-hamas-officials-killed-20261127', 'news', 'Global',
'Israel kills three senior Hamas officials in Gaza strikes',
'Israel kills three senior Hamas officials in Gaza strikes',
'Israel killed three senior Hamas officials in Gaza in three days, including two internal security chiefs, in strikes that have strained the fragile ceasefire.',
'Israel killed three senior Hamas officials in Gaza in three days, including two internal security chiefs, in strikes that have strained the fragile ceasefire.',
$$<p>Israel killed three senior Hamas officials in Gaza in three days this week, including two successive heads of the group's internal security apparatus, in a series of targeted strikes that have put the fragile ceasefire under strain.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>First killing</td><td>Tuesday 24 November: Naeem Abu Nahal, head of internal security for all Gaza</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second killing</td><td>Wednesday 25 November: Muhammad Abu al-Aouf, northern Gaza security chief, with his wife and two daughters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third killing</td><td>Thursday 26 November: Khaled Abu Nada, deputy education minister and economy head</td></tr>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Gaza City, Al-Shati camp and Deir al-Balah</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>On Tuesday Israeli forces and the Shin Bet carried out a strike which resulted in the death of Naeem Muhammad Ashour Abu Nahal, the head of Hamas internal security in Gaza. PM Netanyahu and Defence Minister Katz confirmed the killing. Abu Nahal was in charge of counter-espionage, the protection of senior figures and the running of investigations into security and public order as per the <a href="https://www.jpost.com/israel-news/defense-news/article-916167">Jerusalem Post</a>.</p>

<p>On Wednesday an Israeli strike on an apartment in the Al-Shati refugee camp in western Gaza City killed Muhammad Abu al-Aouf, head of the internal security service in northern Gaza, along with his wife and two daughters, aged 13 and 15, <a href="https://www.dawn.com/news/2047123/israeli-strike-kills-hamas-official-wife-and-daughters">Dawn</a> reported. The Israeli military confirmed the killing of Abu al-Aouf, describing him as a very important figure who was a major source of knowledge in the effort to limit the scope of Israeli counter-terrorism operations. The civil defence said 12 others were wounded.</p>

<p>On Thursday, the military struck and killed Khaled Abu Nada, a senior member of Hamas's political bureau and its deputy minister of education, in a strike on Deir al-Balah in central Gaza. The military said Abu Nada oversaw Hamas's economic portfolio and managed its banking and investment network, directing funds towards militant activities, according to <a href="https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/israeli-strike-kills-hamas-security-official-family-in-gaza-12380154/amp/1">NDTV</a>. Medics said the strike killed Abu Nada and his wife, and at least nine Palestinians were killed overnight in total.</p>

<h2>What the Hamas officials' killings mean</h2>

<p>The three killings in three nights mark an intensification of Israel's campaign of targeted assassinations in Gaza, even as the Trump administration urges Netanyahu to proceed with the US-brokered ceasefire plan, according to <a href="https://www.cbc.bb/news/international-news/israel-intensifies-assassinations-in-gaza-as-us-urges-netanyahu-to-proceed-with-ceasefire-plan/">CBC, reporting CNN</a>. The military said each assassination was a blow to Hamas's leadership capabilities. Still, the group has firmly entrenched its power in the half of Gaza not occupied by the Israeli military. Netanyahu said Israel has killed about 80 Hamas militants in the past month, and that there was no escape for Hamas from his country's long reach.</p>

<p>Hamas chief Khalil al-Hayya has suggested that the group could carry out a retaliatory strike against Israel and thus bring the ceasefire, raising the risk that the week's strikes unravel the truce. The killings also remove the officials who were in charge of Hamas' internal security and its finances at the same time, which Israel has framed as a significant blow to the group's ability to function. Hamas have confirmed the deaths but have made no comment on the allegations regarding their roles. More on the region in our Abu Dhabi <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ukraine-peace-talks-abu-dhabi-20261125">Ukraine peace talks report from Abu Dhabi</a> report, and the latest from around the world in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say whether Hamas will respond or whether the ceasefire will actually last. They don't give a full picture of the casualty figures over the three nights of strikes beyond the medics' reports and they don't say whether the US was consulted prior to the strikes. There is no mention of who will replace the three officials or whether any further targeted killings are on the cards.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.jpost.com/israel-news/defense-news/article-916167">Jerusalem Post</a> for the three killings and the military's account of their roles; <a href="https://www.dawn.com/news/2047123/israeli-strike-kills-hamas-official-wife-and-daughters">Dawn</a> for the Al-Shati strike and the family deaths; <a href="https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/israeli-strike-kills-hamas-security-official-family-in-gaza-12380154/amp/1">NDTV</a> for the Deir al-Balah strike and Netanyahu's figure of 80 killed; <a href="https://www.cbc.bb/news/international-news/israel-intensifies-assassinations-in-gaza-as-us-urges-netanyahu-to-proceed-with-ceasefire-plan/">CBC</a> for the ceasefire context and the retaliation warning.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Who was killed in the Gaza strikes this week?</h3>
<p>Naeem Abu Nahal (Hamas internal security head for all of Gaza) was killed on 24th November; Muhammad Abu al-Aouf (security chief in the north of Gaza) together with his wife and two daughters was killed on the 25th November; and Khaled Abu Nada (deputy minister of education and economy) was killed on the 26th November.</p>

<h3>Is the Gaza ceasefire still standing?</h3>
<p>The ceasefire has not technically been ended, but Hamas has indicated it may respond and end the truce following the three nights of strikes. What happens next is very much in doubt.</p>

<h3>Why is Israel targeting Hamas officials?</h3>
<p>Israel says the strikes are targeted killings of senior figures who were in charge of Hamas' internal security and finances and that each one is a significant blow to the group's leadership.</p>
$$,
