INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'jazan-refinery-attack-nepali-worker-killed-20261127', 'news', 'Saudi Arabia',
'Jazan refinery attack kills Nepali worker',
'Jazan refinery attack kills Nepali worker',
'Jazan refinery attack on Thursday killed a Nepali worker and injured several others, Saudi Arabia said, as Nepal confirmed one citizen dead and three injured.',
'Jazan refinery attack on Thursday killed a Nepali worker and injured several others, Saudi Arabia said, as Nepal confirmed one citizen dead and three injured.',
$$<p>Jazan refinery attack on Thursday 26 November killed a Nepali worker. Several others were injured after a hostile aerial projectile hit the Saudi Aramco refinery on the Red Sea coast and set off a fire.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Jazan refinery, southwestern Saudi Arabia, on the Red Sea coast</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>Thursday 26 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Killed</td><td>1 Nepali worker</td></tr>
<tr><td>Injured</td><td>Several people of various nationalities; Nepal says 3 Nepalis injured</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refinery</td><td>Saudi Aramco, processes up to 400,000 barrels of crude oil a day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Hostile aerial projectile; Saudi-led coalition blames the Houthis</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The projectile hit the refinery on Thursday, sparking a fire and temporarily halting some operations, Saudi Arabia's Energy Ministry said in a statement carried by the Saudi Press Agency, according to <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/26/attack-on-saudi-aramcos-jazan-refinery-kills-worker-disrupts-operations?traffic_source=rss">Al Jazeera</a>. The ministry said the worker who died was Nepali, while the injured were of various nationalities and were receiving medical care. Emergency personnel and specialist technical teams went to the site to assess the damage and check that the facilities were safe. The refinery, near the Yemeni border in the kingdom's south-west, is designed to process up to 400,000 barrels of crude oil a day.</p>

<p>The Saudi-led coalition fighting Yemen's Houthis said the Iranian-backed group had carried out the attack. Coalition spokesman Turki al-Maliki posted on X that the Jazan refinery was attacked by the Houthi militia, and warned that targeting civilians and civilian infrastructure would be met with a firm military response. Later on Thursday, the coalition said its forces had destroyed a Houthi ballistic missile launch platform in Yemen's Hodeidah governorate that was used to stage the attack. There was no immediate statement from the Houthis about the strike, <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/26/attack-on-saudi-aramcos-jazan-refinery-kills-worker-disrupts-operations?traffic_source=rss">Al Jazeera</a> reported.</p>

<p>The strike came a day after the Houthis claimed attacks on Ras Tanura, the kingdom's largest refinery and a major crude export hub, and the Red Sea ports of Jeddah and Jazan. The Houthis also attacked Jazan port on Wednesday, causing minor damage and no casualties, and attempted strikes on Jeddah Islamic Port and the kingdom's western region, the coalition said. On Tuesday, an attack on a tower in Riyadh's King Abdullah Financial District injured 11 people, according to Saudi Civil Defense. The same day, two Italian workers were killed in an attack on a facility in Kuwait, which was blamed on Iran, <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/saudi-arabia/houthi-attack-on-saudi-arabias-jazan-refinery-kills-nepali-worker-3014153">Arab News</a> reported. The Houthis, who control the most populous areas of Yemen, have launched missiles and drones at Saudi airports, oil facilities and office buildings since the country's civil war reignited in July. The World Health Organization reported that 2,361 people had been killed and 10,982 injured since fighting in Yemen escalated in August, with more than 360,000 forced to flee their homes.</p>

<h2>What the Jazan refinery attack means for Nepali workers</h2>

<p>Nepal's Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on X on Thursday that it was deeply saddened by the tragic loss of a Nepali citizen in the recent attacks in Saudi Arabia, and remained concerned for three other Nepali nationals injured in the attacks. One of the injured had recovered, while the other two were undergoing medical treatment, the ministry said. It extended condolences to the bereaved family, wished the injured a speedy recovery, and said civilians and public infrastructure must not be targeted under any pretext. Nepal urges maximum restraint and calls on all concerned to adopt peaceful means for settling disputes and resolving conflicts, the ministry added.</p>

<p>It is the second Nepali worker killed in Saudi Arabia in just over five weeks. On 21 October, Bharat Mahato, 45, from Mahottari district, was killed when debris from an intercepted drone fell at Saudi Aramco's Shaybah oil field in the Eastern Province, according to the <a href="https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/11/05/saudi-firm-to-pay-181-000-riyals-to-family-of-nepali-killed-in-drone-strike">Kathmandu Post</a>. Mahato had worked as a cleaner at a Saudi Aramco project for 14 years. A Filipino worker was also killed in the same incident. His employer has since agreed to pay 181,000 Saudi riyals, more than Rs7.2 million, including end-of-service benefits and compensation, to his family. Nepal's foreign ministry condemned that attack on 22 October.</p>

<p>The death is a reminder of the risks faced by the many Nepalis working in Saudi Arabia and the Gulf, including on oil-field and construction sites. More on energy incidents in the region is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/strait-of-hormuz-oil-tankers-fire-20261124">Strait of Hormuz oil tankers fire</a> report, and the latest news from Nepal is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taplejung-earthquake-20261124">Taplejung earthquake</a> report, with the wider news in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>Neither Saudi Arabia nor Nepal has named the worker who died and Nepal's foreign ministry statement merely refers to "recent attacks" without actually saying which of those attacks led to the fatality (the <a href="https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/11/26/nepal-mourns-citizen-killed-in-saudi-attacks-urges-restraint">Kathmandu Post</a> noted this). The sources provide no overall figure for the number of injured other than "several" of various nationalities; Nepal's figure of three is restricted to Nepalis. The Houthis have made no comment on the Jazan attack. The sources give no details as to the extent of the damage to the refinery or when operations will be restored to full capacity (the damage assessment is still underway). There is no information as to whether the attack in Kuwait on the same day (which resulted in the deaths of two Italian workers) is in any way connected to the Jazan attack.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/26/attack-on-saudi-aramcos-jazan-refinery-kills-worker-disrupts-operations?traffic_source=rss">Al Jazeera</a> for the attack, the death toll, the coalition's attribution and the wider Yemen context; <a href="https://www.arabnews.com/saudi-arabia/houthi-attack-on-saudi-arabias-jazan-refinery-kills-nepali-worker-3014153">Arab News</a> for the Energy Ministry statement, the refinery's capacity and the Kuwait attack; the <a href="https://www.deccanchronicle.com/world/nepal-condoles-death-of-citizen-killed-in-houthi-attack-on-saudi-oil-refinery-urges-restraint-2008388">Deccan Chronicle</a> for the Nepal foreign ministry statement and the three injured Nepalis; the <a href="https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/11/26/nepal-mourns-citizen-killed-in-saudi-attacks-urges-restraint">Kathmandu Post</a> for the Shaybah case, the compensation and the fact that the deceased worker has not been named.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Who was killed in the Jazan refinery attack?</h3>
<p>A Nepali worker at Saudi Aramco's Jazan refinery. His name has not been released by Saudi Arabia or Nepal.</p>

<h3>How many Nepalis were injured?</h3>
<p>Three, according to Nepal's foreign ministry. One has recovered and two are still in treatment. The total number of injured (of all nationalities) has not been disclosed.</p>

<h3>Who does Saudi Arabia blame for the attack?</h3>
<p>The Houthis. The Saudi-led coalition said the Iranian-backed group carried out the strike and said it destroyed the Houthi missile launch platform in Hodeidah used in the attack. The Houthis have not commented.</p>

<h3>Has a Nepali worker been killed in Saudi Arabia previously?</h3>
<p>Yes. Bharat Mahato (45) from Mahottari was killed on 21 October at the Shaybah oil field when debris from a drone which had been intercepted fell on the site. His employer has agreed to pay his family 181,000 Saudi riyals.</p>

<h3>What was the Government of Nepal's position?</h3>
<p>The foreign ministry expressed sorrow, extended condolences to the family, and urged maximum restraint and a peaceful settlement, saying civilians and public infrastructure must not be targeted.</p>
