VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'mississippi-plane-crash-six-killed-horn-island-20261127', 'news', 'USA',
'Six family members killed in Mississippi plane crash',
'Six family members killed in Mississippi plane crash',
'Mississippi plane crash killed six members of two Florida families when their Cessna crashed off Horn Island on 24 November after a fishing trip in Louisiana.',
'Mississippi plane crash killed six members of two Florida families when their Cessna crashed off Horn Island on 24 November after a fishing trip in Louisiana.',
$$<p>Mississippi plane crash killed six members of two Florida families when their small plane crashed off the Mississippi coast on Tuesday 24th November after a fishing trip in Louisiana to mark the start of the holiday season.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Aircraft</td><td>Cessna Centurion 210, single engine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Route</td><td>New Orleans Lakefront Airport to Page Field, Fort Myers, Florida</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crash site</td><td>About 2 miles north-east of Horn Island, Mississippi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>Tuesday 24 November 2026, about 3.30pm local time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Killed</td><td>Six, including two children aged 10 and 12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Investigation</td><td>National Transportation Safety Board</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The plane took off from New Orleans Lakefront Airport on Tuesday afternoon en route to Page Field in Fort Myers and went dark from the tracking systems 34 minutes later, <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/us/news-today/article/gulf-of-mexico-plane-crash-mississippi-9v25vtcpc">The Times</a> reported. Flight tracking showed the Cessna at approximately 8,800 feet just south of Pascagoula, MS, before it suddenly began to dive towards the water, according to <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/us/plane-crash-mississippi-horn-island?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a>. The FAA lost contact with the aircraft at about 3.45pm having been reported to be descending at 10,000 feet per minute.</p>

<p>The US Coast Guard led a four-hour air and sea search, with a US Air Force C-130 Hercules, the FBI and divers from the Mississippi Department of Marine Resources. Crews found debris, a life raft and life jackets floating on the water, and divers then located the remains of all six people inside the submerged fuselage, the Coast Guard said. Commander Trevor Blount, the Coast Guard search and rescue mission coordinator, called it a heartbreaking outcome and offered the deepest sympathies to the families.</p>

<p>The victims were identified by their families and the Jackson County coroner as pilot Brent Stokes, 50, and his son Hunter Stokes, 16; Stokes's sister Arlyn Knox, 43, her husband Michael Knox, 43, and their sons Charles Holden Knox, 12, and Truett Knox, 10, according to <a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2026/11/25/mississippi-plane-crash-horn-island/92955580007/">USA Today</a>. The families said in a joint statement that the group died doing what they loved, celebrating the start of a wonderful holiday season with a fishing trip together before heading home.</p>

<h2>What the Mississippi plane crash means</h2>

<p>The crash has left two Fort Myers families grieving six relatives at the start of the Thanksgiving holiday, including two children, and it is the second fatal small-plane accident in the Gulf region to draw national attention this month. The National Transportation Safety Board is leading the investigation, with the FAA assisting, and investigators planned to travel to the area to document and examine the wreckage once recovery was complete. The NTSB has asked witnesses, or anyone with surveillance video or information, to contact it.</p>

<p>All we know so far is that it raises more questions than it answers. The aircraft in question is a six seater Cessna, built in 1972, had just done a very short hop at Lakefront from Buras (near Venice, LA) and was about 30 minutes into its flight home when it went down. The rate of descent as per the tracking data and the FAA's account are at odds with each other. Flightradar24 has it at 18,000 ft/min in the final seconds, the FAA has it at 10,000 ft/min. More on holiday travel disruption in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/delta-flight-delays-thanksgiving-outage-20261124">Delta flight delays</a> report and the wider world in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say what caused the crash, and the NTSB has not issued a preliminary cause. There's no mention of whether the pilot reported any problem prior to contact being lost or whether the weather was a factor. There's also no information as to when the NTSB will issue its preliminary report or when the wreckage will be recovered from the water.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/us/plane-crash-mississippi-horn-island?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the flight path, the search and the families' statement; <a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2026/11/25/mississippi-plane-crash-horn-island/92955580007/">USA Today</a> for the victims' names and the NTSB investigation; <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/us/news-today/article/gulf-of-mexico-plane-crash-mississippi-9v25vtcpc">The Times</a> for the take-off, the crash time and the aircraft's age; <a href="https://people.com/6-family-members-were-returning-home-from-fishing-trip-when-they-were-killed-in-plane-crash-12280619">People</a> for the coroner's identification and the fishing trip background.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Who died in the Mississippi plane crash?</h3>
<p>Brent Stokes, 50, and his son Hunter, 16; Arlyn Knox, 43; her husband, Michael Knox, 43; and their sons, Charles Holden Knox, 12, and Truett Knox, 10. All six were from Fort Myers, Florida.</p>

<h3>Where did the plane crash?</h3>
<p>Approximately two miles northeast of Horn Island (a barrier island off the Mississippi coast), in the gulf waters between the island and the mainland, on Tuesday 24th November 2026.</p>

<h3>What caused the crash?</h3>
<p>The cause is not yet known. The NTSB is leading the investigation with the FAA providing assistance.</p>
