<h2>What the New Zealand election results mean</h2>

<p>The hung parliament is a very sharp about-turn on the election night picture where Luxon's coalition was looking so secure and it actually gives real power to a party that didn't exist in Parliament prior to this election. Opportunity, the centrist party whose whole platform is around a universal basic income funded by a land value tax, has said it would walk away from any deal if its five 'bottom lines' weren't met (including lobbying reform and eventually the total elimination of bottom trawling) and those positions are not at all shared by ACT or NZ First according to the <a href="https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-opportunity-to-choose-between-re-elected-coalition-or-second-election-after-final-results/ZTWOVRCXOBHFDC5NWHGJ5CJERQ/">New Zealand Herald</a>.</p>

<p>If no deal is reached then NZ could go back to the polls (only weeks after the 7 November vote). Luxon will also have to appoint new caretaker ministers by 3 December to cover the roles of those who are no longer MPs (Nicola Willis, Chris Bishop and Paul Goldsmith). More on the region in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/australia-aps-jobs-november-2026-20261121">Australia APS jobs November 2026</a> report and the wider world in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources have any information as to whether Opportunity will support the coalition, will abstain or will force a second election. The parties have had only very preliminary staff discussions to date. There is no information as to whether the Wairarapa and Hamilton East results (Labour winning in Wairarapa by 81 votes) will be challenged in recounts. There is no information as to when a government could be formed, though it is increasingly unlikely to be formed by Christmas.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/new-zealands-ruling-coalition-falls-just-short-majority-final-election-count-2026-11-27/">Reuters</a> for the final seat counts and the vote totals; <a href="https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2820329/election-2026-final-results-coalition-loses-majority-another-election-possible">RNZ</a> for the hung parliament, the recount window and the caretaker arrangements; <a href="https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-opportunity-to-choose-between-re-elected-coalition-or-second-election-after-final-results/ZTWOVRCXOBHFDC5NWHGJ5CJERQ/">New Zealand Herald</a> for Opportunity's options and bottom lines; <a href="https://asia.nikkei.com/politics/new-zealand-ruling-coalition-misses-majority-as-election-results-announced">Nikkei Asia</a> for the universal basic income platform.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Who won the New Zealand election?</h3>
<p>No bloc has a majority. The governing coalition of National, ACT and NZ First has 65 of the 130 seats (one short of the 66 needed) and the centre-left bloc has 58. The Opportunity Party, with seven seats, holds the balance of power.</p>

<h3>Will there be another election in New Zealand?</h3>
<p>It is possible. If Opportunity doesn't back the coalition or agree to abstain then we go back to the polls. There's no set date for a deal, but Parliament has to be in by 14th January.</p>

<h3>What is the Opportunity Party?</h3>
<p>A new centrist party led by Qiulae Wong, which won seven seats on a platform of universal basic income (funded by a land value tax), lobbying reform and the eventual banning of bottom trawling.</p>
