VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'poundland-administration-jobs-at-risk-20261127', 'news', 'UK',
'Poundland administration puts 11,000 jobs at risk',
'Poundland administration puts 11,000 jobs at risk',
'Poundland administration puts about 11,000 jobs at risk, as MPs gave owner Gordon Brothers until noon on Friday to respond.',
'Poundland administration puts about 11,000 jobs at risk, as MPs gave owner Gordon Brothers until noon on Friday to respond.',
$$<p>Poundland administration commenced on Wednesday 25th November, putting around 11,000 jobs at risk across some 600 stores in the UK and Ireland. This follows the failure of owner Gordon Brothers to secure a buyer for the business as a whole.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>Poundland Limited and Poundland Elgin Limited</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administration date</td><td>Wednesday 25 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administrators</td><td>Clare Kennedy, Daniel Imison and Kevin Wall, AlixPartners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stores</td><td>Around 600 in the UK and Ireland</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jobs at risk</td><td>About 11,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner</td><td>Gordon Brothers, bought Poundland for £1 in June 2025</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Poundland's directors filed for insolvency at the High Court on 25th November and AlixPartners partners Clare Kennedy, Daniel Imison and Kevin Wall have been appointed as joint administrators, according to <a href="https://www.retail-insight-network.com/news/poundland-enters-administration-10000-jobs-risk/">Retail Insight Network</a>. The administrators have taken over the sale process and are now in the process of seeking buyers for the business as a whole or for parts of it and its assets. The shops and distribution centres are all still open and trading in the meantime so the jobs are at risk rather than already gone.</p>

<p>Gordon Brothers (a US investment house) purchased Poundland from Pepco Group on 12 June 2025 for a nominal sum (widely reported as £1), with a commitment to provide up to £80m of financing as part of a turnaround. They started looking for a turnaround, <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/business/companies-markets/article/poundland-collapse-problems-gordon-brothers-jobs-vrq7jmb2h">The Times</a> reported. The firm began seeking a buyer in September 2026 and on 29 October Poundland filed a notice of intention to appoint administrators (giving it temporary protection from creditors whilst it sought a buyer). Gordon Brothers said they had looked at every possible route to a sale of the business and put the years of instability, the rise in costs and the risk of weaker sales.</p>

<p>The collapse comes right up against Christmas, the peak trading period for high street retailers. Poundland has already closed over 100 stores as part of the restructuring under Gordon Brothers and has changed its returns policy with effect from Thursday 26th November.</p>

<h2>What the Poundland administration means</h2>

<p>The admin is the most stark warning yet of the pressure on the UK discount high street with the cost of doing business and the sheer scale of competition from supermarkets and the online sector having effectively squeezed the very cheapest of retailers. For the c.11,000 people in the business the immediate question is whether AlixPartners can find a buyer for the whole chain or whether it will be sold off in parcels (which would almost certainly involve closures). There are reports of other retailers being interested in individual sites which would save some jobs but not the chain as such.</p>

<p>Parliament is now in the mix. The Commons Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee wrote to Gordon Brothers director Mark Newton-Jones on 25 November requesting him to attend a public hearing and giving him until noon on Friday 27 November to respond, according to <a href="https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/365/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee/news/219261/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee-write-to-poundland-owners-gordon-brothers/">UK Parliament</a>. The committee also want assurances that neither Gordon Brothers nor any of its associated parties will act as administrator, liquidator or in any other insolvency capacity for Poundland. Newton-Jones had given evidence in private on 11 November whilst the sale negotiations were still ongoing. More on the wider pressures on British jobs is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/royal-mail-christmas-jobs-22000-temporary-roles-20261116">Royal Mail Christmas jobs</a> report and the latest from the wider world is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say how many stores (if any) will close, or how long the admin sale process will take. There is no mention of any buyer or any offer having been made, nor is there any indication as to whether staff will be paid in full through Christmas. It doesn't say what Newton-Jones will say to the committee or whether the committee will have its public hearing prior to a buyer being identified.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/365/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee/news/219261/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee-write-to-poundland-owners-gordon-brothers/">UK Parliament</a> (committee letter, noon deadline and the private evidence session); <a href="https://www.thetimes.com/business/companies-markets/article/poundland-collapse-problems-gordon-brothers-jobs-vrq7jmb2h">The Times</a> (the £1 purchase, the £80m financing and the failed sale); <a href="https://www.retail-insight-network.com/news/poundland-enters-administration-10000-jobs-risk/">Retail Insight Network</a> (the administrators and 25th November); <a href="https://fish4news.co.uk/poundland-administration/">Fish4News</a> (the stores that are still trading and the change to the returns policy).</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Has Poundland closed down?</h3>
<p>No. Poundland went into administration on 25th November 2026 but its stores and distribution centres are still open and trading as the administrators search for a buyer.</p>

<h3>How many Poundland jobs are at risk?</h3>
<p>About 11,000 jobs are at risk across c.600 stores in the UK and Ireland. They are described as at risk rather than lost as no closures have been announced.</p>

<h3>What's the deadline for Gordon Brothers to get back to the MPs?</h3>
<p>Mark Newton-Jones (director at owner Gordon Brothers) has until noon on Friday 27th November 2026 to respond to the Commons Business Committee's request for him to attend a public hearing.</p>
