<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>"Choosin' Texas" held No. 1 for a record 29th week on the Billboard Hot 100 (billboard chart dated 28 November) according to Billboard's chart report as cited by <a href="https://top40weekly.com/chart-commentary-november-28-2026/">Top40Weekly</a>. In the 13th–19th November tracking week the song had 16.9 m official US streams, 45.5 m radio airplay audience impressions and 6,000 in sales. The same week it reached its 50th week atop the Hot Country Songs chart, equaling Bebe Rexha and Florida Georgia Line's "Meant to Be" for the longest run in that chart's history.</p>

<p>The 29 weeks also puts Langley level with Drake for the most weeks any artist has spent at No. 1 in a single calendar year. Drake achieved that in 2018 with three songs (God's Plan, In My Feelings and Nice for What) and Langley has matched it with just one track, <a href="https://backstagecountry.com/2026/11/25/ella-langleys-choosin-texas-ties-drakes-calendar-year-mark-with-29th-week-at-no-1/">Backstage Country</a> reported. The milestone came just days after Langley had picked up a record six awards at the 60th CMA Awards on 18 November and earned seven Grammy nominations. The song, released in October 2025, first reached the summit in February, became the longest running non-holiday No 1 of all time in early September and subsequently surpassed Mariah Carey's 22 weeks at No 1 that same month. Behind her the Christmas surge is well and truly on: Mariah Carey's All I Want for Christmas Is You has jumped from 18 to 4, Last Christmas is up to 5 and Jingle Bell Rock is at 9. Our report on the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ella-langley-choosin-texas-ties-drake-hot-100-record-20261125">28th week of the Choosin Texas run</a> was on the previous chart and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/popmusic">Pop Music</a> hub has the rest of the week's chart news.</p>

<h2>What the Ella Langley Choosin Texas 29-week record means</h2>

<p>The Drake comparison really does illustrate the scale of this run. Drake required three separate hits over the course of 2018 to spend 29 weeks at No.1 in a year; Langley has done it with one song which has barely left the top spot since February. The 50 week Hot Country Songs tie is equally telling in that "Meant to Be" was the seminal country crossover of its time and "Choosin' Texas" has now matched that achievement whilst also holding the all time Hot 100 record. The threat is now seasonal rather than musical. Streams and radio both dipped in the most recent tracking week and with three Christmas singles already in the top 10 it's traditionally December that sees "All I Want for Christmas Is You" make its annual run at the top. The next chart will be the first to have a full week of Christmas listening to go against Langley and it will be the first indication of whether the run can survive the Christmas period.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>Nothing in the report indicates whether Langley has any new release, performance or TV appearance scheduled to keep the No.1 spot through December. There's no information as to how the song is performing in terms of streaming outside the US and the sources give no indication as to whether a particular bundle, vinyl release or promotion was the driver of the sales increase. They also don't say when (if ever) Langley plans to release a follow-up single.</p>

