(gen_random_uuid(), 'taylor-swift-showgirl-trademark-lawsuit-enough-is-enough-20261127', 'pop-music', 'USA',
'Taylor Swift Showgirl Lawsuit: Lawyers Say Enough Is Enough',
'Taylor Swift Showgirl Lawsuit: Lawyers Say Enough Is Enough',
'Taylor Swift Showgirl lawsuit sees her lawyers ask a California judge to step in, saying Maren Wade''s arguments to keep it there must end.',
'Taylor Swift Showgirl lawsuit sees her lawyers ask a California judge to step in, saying Maren Wade''s arguments to keep it there must end.',
$$<p>The Taylor Swift Showgirl lawsuit took a sharper turn on Wednesday, 25 November, when the singer's lawyers filed new papers asking a federal judge to intervene and end the fight over whether the case should stay in California. "Here we go again," the filing reads. "Enough is enough."</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Claimant</td><td>Maren Flagg, who performs as Maren Wade, Las Vegas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Album at issue</td><td>The Life of a Showgirl (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim</td><td>Album title and merchandise infringe her "Confessions of a Showgirl" trademark</td></tr>
<tr><td>Filed</td><td>30 March 2026, amended 16 June 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Court</td><td>US District Court, Central District of California</td></tr>
<tr><td>Judge</td><td>Serena R. Murillo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Next hearings</td><td>Discovery dispute 9 December 2026; dismissal motion 3 February 2027</td></tr>
<tr><td>Defendants</td><td>Swift, TAS Rights Management, UMG Recordings, Bravado</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Swift's lawyers are opposing Wade's latest arguments for keeping the case in California, according to new court docs that TMZ obtained on Wednesday, 25 November, the <a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/music/news/taylor-swifts-the-life-of-a-showgirl-lawsuit-takes-a-new-turn-as-lawyers-declare-enough-is-enough/articleshow/136179684.cms">Times of India</a> reported. Their position is that Swift has no nexus to California and that performing in the state isn't sufficient to be sued there. Swift is a resident of Tennessee according to her legal team. Wade has cited Swift's performances and appearances in the state, including her appearance at the Academy Museum Gala in October where she performed a song from Toy Story 5, as reasons the case belongs in California.</p>

<p>The dispute dates back to March when Wade (real name Maren Flagg) brought action against Swift, TAS Rights Management, UMG Recordings and Bravado (the merchandise company), claiming that the branding of the 2025 album and its associated merchandise infringes her existing trademark. Wade has been writing a column entitled "Confessions of a Showgirl" for Las Vegas Weekly since 2014, which has developed into a live cabaret show, a book and a podcast. The mark was registered in 2015, according to <a href="https://brandprotector.com/news/taylor-swift-showgirl-trademark-lawsuit">BrandProtector</a>. She is seeking damages (including profits from the brand) and an injunction preventing Swift from using the album name. Swift's side have dismissed the claims, called the lawsuit meritless, and previously asked for it to be dropped, arguing that the album is a work of creativity which is protected by law as is its promotional merchandise.</p>

<p>The case has also become a fight over evidence. In October, Wade's lawyers asked to depose Swift for up to three hours and a TAS Rights Management representative for up to four hours, and Swift's team wants discovery paused while the court considers the motion to dismiss. A hearing on the discovery dispute is set for 9 December, and the dismissal motion is scheduled for 3 February 2027, <a href="https://www.billionaires.africa/2026/11/25/billionaire-singer-taylor-swift-asks-judge-to-intervene-in-vegas-showgirls-lawsuit/">Billionaires Africa</a> reported. Separately, the US Patent and Trademark Office has twice issued non-final refusals on TAS Rights Management's application to register "The Life of a Showgirl", in November 2025 and September 2026, and the application is still pending. Our earlier report on the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taylor-swift-showgirl-trademark-second-refusal-20261026">second trademark refusal for The Life of a Showgirl</a> covered that part of the story, and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/popmusic">Pop Music</a> hub has the rest of the week's Taylor Swift news.</p>

<h2>What the Taylor Swift Showgirl lawsuit means</h2>

<p>The new filing is purely a venue filing (not about who's right on the mark itself) but venue is going to be a massive issue. If Judge Murillo agrees that a California court has no jurisdiction over Swift and TAS Rights Management then the California case is over and Wade would have to go elsewhere (presumably Tennessee as that's where Swift says she lives). That's why the language in the filing is so direct. Swift's side are essentially describing Wade's various arguments as a waste of the Court's time. Wade's side are describing it as a small working performer trying to defend a brand she's built over a decade against one of the biggest acts in the world. No confusion or infringement, and there has been no ruling on the injunction request or the dismissal motion. So the substance of the mark claim is still completely untested.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the reports indicate when the judge will rule on the new filing or whether the 9th December discovery hearing will proceed as scheduled. There's no information as to how much Wade is seeking in damages or whether either side has even discussed a settlement. The reports are also silent as to whether Swift's side will go any further with the case if it is dismissed in California or what the position is on the pending trademark application while the action is ongoing.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/music/news/taylor-swifts-the-life-of-a-showgirl-lawsuit-takes-a-new-turn-as-lawyers-declare-enough-is-enough/articleshow/136179684.cms">The Times of India</a> for the new filing and the TMZ papers; <a href="https://www.justjared.com/2026/11/25/taylor-swift-life-of-a-showgirl-lawsuit-enough-is-enough/">Just Jared</a> for the filing wording and the Academy Museum Gala point; <a href="https://brandprotector.com/news/taylor-swift-showgirl-trademark-lawsuit">BrandProtector</a> for the case history, the trademark registration and the hearing dates; <a href="https://www.billionaires.africa/2026/11/25/billionaire-singer-taylor-swift-asks-judge-to-intervene-in-vegas-showgirls-lawsuit/">Billionaires Africa</a> for the deposition fight and the court details.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Who is suing Taylor Swift over The Life of a Showgirl?</h3>
<p>Las Vegas performer Maren Flagg (who performs as Maren Wade) has said the album title and associated merchandise infringe her 'Confessions of a Showgirl' brand which she registered as a trademark in 2015.</p>
<h3>What did Taylor Swift's lawyers say in the new filing?</h3>
<p>In papers obtained by TMZ on 25 November they're asking a federal judge to step in, saying "Here we go again. Enough is enough". They maintain the California court has no jurisdiction as Swift is a Tennessee resident and performing in the state is not enough to be brought there.</p>
<h3>When are the next hearings in the case?</h3>
<p>A hearing on the discovery dispute is set for 9th December 2026 and the motion to dismiss is set for 3rd February 2027.</p>
