-- gemini-live-guided-vision-iphone-20261127 (tech-ai; Global; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'gemini-live-guided-vision-iphone-20261127', 'tech-ai', 'Global',
'Gemini Live Guided Vision reaches iPhone users',
'Gemini Live Guided Vision reaches iPhone users',
'Gemini Live Guided Vision iPhone rollout brings Google''s camera-based spoken guidance to iOS, after its Android debut on 1 October 2026.',
'Gemini Live Guided Vision iPhone rollout brings Google''s camera-based spoken guidance to iOS, after its Android debut on 1 October 2026.',
$$<p>Gemini Live Guided Vision has made it to iPhone users, extending the camera-based spoken guidance feature beyond Android for the first time. Google announced the iOS rollout via its official Gemini app account on 25th November 2026, two months after the feature had launched on Android.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>Google</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feature</td><td>Guided Vision in Gemini Live</td></tr>
<tr><td>iPhone rollout</td><td>Announced 25 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Android debut</td><td>1 October 2026, Android 9 and later</td></tr>
<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Open Live, point the camera, get spoken descriptions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Built with</td><td>Blind and low-vision community; tested with Aira</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limits</td><td>Not for navigation, obstacle detection or mobility aid use</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Guided Vision turns the phone's camera into a talking assistant within Gemini Live. The user opens a Live session, shares the camera and asks questions about what the camera sees. Gemini then describes the scene to the user and will answer any follow up questions in the course of the same conversation. If the camera isn't correctly oriented then Gemini will be able to tell the user to pan slowly to the right, to tilt down or to step back, according to <a href="https://www.gadgets360.com/ai/news/google-gemini-live-guided-vision-ios-roll-out-12379552?pfrom=home-ndtvworld_world_gadgets&technology">Gadgets360</a>.</p>

<p>Google built the feature with input from blind and low vision users and worked with Aira (the visual interpreting service for the blind and low vision) to train Gemini Live on real world conversation. The Aira Trusted Tester network (over 1,000 testers) was used to test the feature and assess its safety. Google's own examples are things like reading the tiny print on food packaging, working out the setting on a washing machine, finding a dropped earbud and working out whether a striped shirt goes with a pair of trousers.</p>

<p>The iPhone announcement was brief, and Google has left out several details. It has not specified which iOS version or iPhone models are required, which countries and languages get the feature on iPhone, or whether any subscription is needed. Its help page still covers only the Android setup, and no dedicated iPhone guide has been published. A report from Japanese publication Jetstream says the switch lives in the Voice section of the Gemini app's settings. Still, Google has not confirmed iPhone setup steps, so that route remains unconfirmed, <a href="https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/gemini-lives-guided-vision-now-works-on-iphone-but-google-left-out-a-few-details/">Digital Trends</a> reported. The rollout is also gradual, so the feature may not appear for every iPhone user straight away.</p>

<p>Google cautions that Guided Vision can make mistakes and should not be considered a medical device, a mobility aid or a replacement for a white cane. It should not be used for navigation, safe travel guidance or obstacle detection. The same feature will also be supported by Google's new Gemini-powered smart glasses, which go on sale on 10 December.</p>

<h2>What the Gemini Live Guided Vision iPhone rollout means</h2>

<p>This is accessibility tech finally making it to the platform where it was previously absent. Guided Vision launched on Android on 1 October and iPhone users (who could already talk to Gemini via the app and, from earlier this month, via Apple CarPlay) were previously unable to point the camera and have it talk them through what it sees. The iPhone launch finally closes that gap and it comes as Google is pushing Gemini into glasses and cars as well as phones so the same spoken guidance will be available on the face as well as in the hand.</p>

<p>The very fact that it's a thin announcement is the story. Google haven't said where the iPhone version works, what it requires or how you turn it on, which suggests it's still in the stage of being rolled out rather than complete. For the blind and low vision users it's important that it's being framed honestly: it's a conversational helper for reading labels and finding things, not a navigation tool and Google make that perfectly clear. The value of the feature will be determined by how accurately it describes the world in front of the camera and how quickly the iPhone version comes to the fore. More on Google's AI hardware in our coverage of the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/google-gemini-smart-glasses-preorder-395-20261117">Gemini smart glasses coverage</a>, and the wider field in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't say what iOS version or iPhone models Guided Vision is required on, which countries and languages are included in the iPhone rollout or when it will be available to those who don't have it yet. Google haven't said whether Guided Vision requires a Gemini subscription or is available on the free tier. The sources don't say anything about the accuracy of Guided Vision or its performance in low light, in a busy scene or with moving objects. The sources don't say whether the iPhone version will have the accessibility shortcuts and TalkBack integration that the Android version has or when a dedicated iPhone help page will be available.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.gadgets360.com/ai/news/google-gemini-live-guided-vision-ios-roll-out-12379552?pfrom=home-ndtvworld_world_gadgets&technology">Gadgets360</a> for the iOS rollout, the 25 November announcement and the camera reframing cues; <a href="https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/gemini-lives-guided-vision-now-works-on-iphone-but-google-left-out-a-few-details/">Digital Trends</a> for the Android debut on 1 October, the Aira partnership, the missing iPhone details and the smart glasses date; <a href="https://www.digit.in/news/general/guided-vision-in-gemini-live-now-rolling-out-on-ios-here-is-how-it-can-assist-iphone-users-in-real-time.html">Digit</a> for the Android 9 requirement, the follow-up questions and the rollout timing.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How do I use Guided Vision on the iPhone?</h3>
<p>Open Gemini Live, point your camera at what you're interested in and just follow the on-screen and spoken instructions. Google haven't published iPhone setup instructions and it's a gradual rollout so it may not be in your app yet. You'll need to update the Gemini app from the App Store first.</p>

<h3>Can Guided Vision replace the white cane?</h3>
<p>No. Google state that Guided Vision is prone to error and is not a medical device, not a mobility aid and not a replacement for a white cane. It should not be used for navigation, safe travel or for obstacle detection.</p>
