(gen_random_uuid(), 'spacex-starmind-fcc-approval-million-satellites-20261127', 'tech-ai', 'USA',
'SpaceX Starmind wins FCC approval for 1m AI satellites',
'SpaceX Starmind wins FCC approval for 1m AI satellites',
'SpaceX Starmind won FCC approval on 25 November to deploy up to one million satellites as orbital AI data centres, with the first due by 2033.',
'SpaceX Starmind won FCC approval on 25 November to deploy up to one million satellites as orbital AI data centres, with the first due by 2033.',
$$<p>The FCC has approved SpaceX to deploy up to one million satellites as part of its SpaceX Starmind orbital AI data centre network. The order was adopted and issued on 25 November 2026. This is the first time the US has licensed a constellation of data centres in space.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Regulator</td><td>Federal Communications Commission, Space Bureau</td></tr>
<tr><td>Order</td><td>DA 26-1273, adopted and released 25 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Satellites</td><td>Up to 1,000,000, in six orbital shells</td></tr>
<tr><td>Orbit</td><td>500 to 1,002 km, 30-degree and sun-synchronous inclinations</td></tr>
<tr><td>First satellite</td><td>By November 2033</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full constellation</td><td>By November 2040</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compute hardware</td><td>Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The FCC's Space Bureau granted SpaceX's application in part and deferred the rest, under order DA 26-1273. The grant covers a non-geostationary constellation of up to one million satellites operating as data centres in low Earth orbit, at altitudes between 500 and 1,002 kilometres. SpaceX filed the application on 30 January 2026, and the FCC said the grant serves the public interest by letting SpaceX pioneer technology that advances American leadership in space and artificial intelligence. FCC Chair Brendan Carr said the decision had cleared the way for the network, and the FCC said deploying the constellation would help meet rapidly growing demand for AI computing power, <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/26/business/spacex-satellites-space-data-centers-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> reported.</p>

<p>The licence comes with a strict timetable. SpaceX must have its first Starmind satellite operating by November 2033, then 100,000 satellites, or 10 per cent of the total, by November 2035, and 500,000 by November 2038, with the full million due by November 2040. If SpaceX misses a milestone, the authorisation becomes void. The scale is vast: the Starmind constellation would increase the total number of satellites in Earth orbit by roughly 50-fold, and there are only about 17,000 satellites in orbit today.</p>

<p>Each satellite is effectively a flying AI server. SpaceX announced in August that it was partnering with Nvidia to design the compute payload, and each Starmind satellite will include Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs for data-centre-class space compute, according to <a href="https://www.geekwire.com/2026/fcc-clears-spacex-million-satellites-starmind-data-center/">GeekWire</a>. The concept is to capture solar energy in space to power low cost, high performance AI computing for Earth, the satellites being linked by high bandwidth laser comms and the results being sent back to Earth. SpaceX has said it will start flying the Starmind satellites in 2027 and its CFO last week said the first data centres would launch in 2027 with the programme expanding through to 2029.</p>

<p>The approval is not unconditional. The FCC authorised radio frequencies for backup and emergency telemetry, tracking and command in the Ka-band spectrum on an unprotected, non-interference basis as Starmind will be using optical links for its primary communications. The FCC has also found that SpaceX's orbital debris plan is only compliant for satellites below 600km and as four of the six shells are above that line SpaceX will have to file a modification application with a final debris plan prior to launching anything above that altitude.</p>

<h2>What the SpaceX Starmind approval means</h2>

<p>This is a regulatory first, but it's a licence not a launch. No Starmind satellite has flown yet and the FCC deadlines give SpaceX 14 years to build the full constellation which speaks volumes as to how far the concept is from reality. The near term test is whether the first satellites fly in 2027 as SpaceX have told investors and whether the Nvidia-powered AI design works in orbit where the only way heat can get out is by radiation and there's no air to cool the chips.</p>

<p>If it works the prize is huge. Ground based AI data centres are limited by land, power and water for cooling. Starmind would be using the sun as its power source and be able to beam the results down via the Starlink network. That is why the FCC's decision was so much about American leadership in AI and why you've already got Google and Blue Origin are pursuing orbital data centres. The other side of the coin is that you'd have a million satellites and that would have a massive impact on the night sky and would absolutely clog up the most used orbits. The FCC's own order contains a record of public concern about orbital congestion. More on SpaceX's launch programme is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/spacex-starship-flight-15-first-catch-20261119">Starship flight coverage</a> and the wider field is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources do not say how much the constellation will cost, how much computing power a single satellite will deliver in practice, or who the first customers will be. The FCC order does not require a surety bond, and no source puts a figure on the investment needed to build a million satellites. The sources do not say whether SpaceX will actually launch all one million satellites, and several reports note that it remains uncertain. The FCC's decision also says nothing about the environmental review: consistent with its October 2026 order, the FCC will not require further review under the National Environmental Policy Act for the Starmind system. The sources do not say when the first commercial service might begin, only that SpaceX aims to start flying satellites in 2027.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://eu-assets.contentstack.com/v3/assets/blt23eb5bbc4124baa6/bltf4cdf2af47159e12/6b071c5f5506bb38bca5b0ec/FCC_report_and_order_on_SpaceX_Starmind_AI_constellation_11-25-2026.pdf">the FCC order DA 26-1273</a> for the authorisation, the milestones, the orbital shells and the debris condition; <a href="https://www.geekwire.com/2026/fcc-clears-spacex-million-satellites-starmind-data-center/">GeekWire</a> for the Nvidia Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs and the 2027 flight plan; <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/26/business/spacex-satellites-space-data-centers-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the FCC statement on AI computing demand and the deployment deadlines; <a href="https://www.wsj.com/business/fcc-authorizes-spacex-to-build-1-million-data-centers-in-space-0eb20ee7">the Wall Street Journal</a> for FCC Chair Brendan Carr's confirmation and the 2033 and 2040 deadlines.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How many satellites can SpaceX launch under the Starmind approval?</h3>
<p>Up to one million. The FCC authorised a constellation of up to 1,000,000 satellites operating as data centres in low Earth orbit, in six orbital shells between 500 and 1,002 kilometres.</p>

<h3>When must the first Starmind satellite launch?</h3>
<p>The licence requires the first operational satellite by November 2033, then 100,000 by November 2035, 500,000 by November 2038 and the full million by November 2040. SpaceX itself has said it plans to start flying Starmind satellites in 2027.</p>
